<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">256 BCE – 120 BCE (approx.) · capital Bactra</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The easternmost Hellenistic kingdom, where Greek kings ruled Bactria's 'thousand cities' and struck some of the finest coins of the ancient world.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great's</a> conquest. Around 256 BCE its governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodotus-i">Diodotus I</a> broke away, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodotus-ii">Diodotus II</a> ruled as an independent king. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euthydemus-i">Euthydemus I</a> then seized the throne and withstood a two-year siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Bactra</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a>, who recognised him as king around 206 BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Greek and Macedonian settlers formed the ruling class over a largely Iranian population, with cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ai-khanoum">Ai-Khanoum</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> featuring a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> and palace in Greek style. Irrigated farming and trade supported the state. Under Demetrius I the kings crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek</a> kingdoms, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucratides-i">Eucratides I</a> ruled a vast territory in the 2nd century BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucratides-i">Eucratides</a> was murdered around 145 BCE, nomad invasions by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saka">Saka</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ai-khanoum">Ai-Khanoum</a> was abandoned. Greek rule continued south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek</a> kingdoms until about 10 CE, and Greek script lived on in Kushan coinage.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/greco-bactrian-kingdom.jpg" alt="Greco-Bactrian Kingdom" width="320" height="218"><figcaption>Image: World Imaging · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greco-BactrianKingdomMap.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>256 BCE – 120 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bactra</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Diodotus I, Euthydemus I, Eucratides I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion, Zoroastrianism, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek, Bactrian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, <a href="/countries/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>, <a href="/countries/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bactria, Graeco-Bactrian Kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-188&amp;focus=greco-bactrian-kingdom" aria-label="Greco-Bactrian Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6360 853 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Greco-Bactrian Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6680 1109L6140 1232M6680 1109L6804 1292"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 1232h0M6804 1292h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6680 1109h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6680 1109h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-188&amp;focus=greco-bactrian-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
